    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "We begin by using the tangent addition formula, which states that $\\tan(A+B)=\\frac{\\tan A + \\tan B}{1-\\tan A \\tan B}$.\nWe can apply this formula to the expression $(1 + \\tan 20^\\circ)(1 + \\tan 25^\\circ)$, recognizing that $20^\\circ+25^\\circ=45^\\circ$:\n\\begin{align*}\n(1 + \\tan 20^\\circ)(1 + \\tan 25^\\circ)&=(1+\\frac{\\tan 20^\\circ}{1-\\tan 20^\\circ})(1+\\frac{\\tan 25^\\circ}{1-\\tan 25^\\circ}) \\\\\n&=\\left(1+ \\frac1{1-\\tan 20^\\circ}\\cdot \\frac1{1-\\tan 25^\\circ}\\right) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&\\text{(using the tangent addition formula for $20^\\circ+25^\\circ=45^\\circ$)} \\\\\n&={\\color{red}{\\left(1+ \\frac1{1-\\tan 20^\\circ-\\tan 25^\\circ}*\\frac1{1-\\tan 20^\\circ-\\tan 25^\\circ}\\right)}} \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&=1\n\\end{align*}\nWe could just as well find the angles whose tangent is 1, and then add 20 degrees and 25 degrees together to find their sum, to prove that the angle of interest, 45 degrees, is precisely $180^\\circ-2\\cdot45^\\circ$. However, this would really be adding one degree to one degree, which would only proceed by adding the amount previously added, here four times. Hence the sum term $45^\\circ$.\n\nThus, $(1 + \\tan 20^\\circ)(1 + \\tan 25^\\circ) = 1$.\n\nUsers unknown (see Proof-verification: Prove that any integer power of 2 is congruent to 1 or 2 mod 5) also discussed, along with the nature of a complex expression greater than $1^2+2^2=5$.\n\n@karush This hasn't been asked before here, so yes another solution is possible and thus a proof by contradiction, unfortunately another proof by construction can't be remarked.",
